WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.500
A target makes too little NGF for every neuron that reaches it.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.560
So which neurons get to live?

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.310
Start with what you already know.

4
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.390
Sympathetic neurons live in small clusters of nerve cells called ganglia.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.920
They send axons to targets such as the eye, the salivary gland and the heart.

6
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.980
Those targets release nerve growth factor, NGF.

7
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.420
The target makes it, not the neurons.

8
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.773
Neurons that get enough NGF live.

9
00:00:33.693 --> 00:00:36.153
Those that do not die through a pro-death gene called Bax.

10
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:39.517
This is normal development, not damage.

11
00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.797
In many developing populations, roughly half the neurons die.

12
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.820
The neurotrophic hypothesis puts it in one line.

13
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.920
Neurons compete for a limiting amount of neurotrophic factor for survival.

14
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.213
Change the target, and the count follows.

15
00:00:56.213 --> 00:01:01.373
Give a chick embryo an extra limb, and fewer motor neurons on that side die.

16
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:06.023
So the size of the target sets roughly how many neurons can win.

17
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.793
But the hypothesis only says there is a contest.

18
00:01:10.713 --> 00:01:12.933
It does not say how the contest is decided.

19
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.243
It also leaves a second question open.

20
00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:20.203
How does the outcome set the fit between a target and its neurons?

21
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:23.987
The answer here comes from one cell type.

22
00:01:23.987 --> 00:01:27.967
Sympathetic neurons of the mouse superior cervical ganglion.

23
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.850
That ganglion sits in the neck and supplies the head.

24
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.810
Some experiments used cultured rat neurons.

25
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.530
Make a guess before going on.

26
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.710
If a target can feed only some neurons, what decides which ones?

27
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.200
Here is the idea this lesson builds.

28
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.320
The target sets how many.

29
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.320
The neurons decide which.

30
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.310
Take neurons that are otherwise alike.

31
00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.210
A small lead can grow only if its signal rises faster than in proportion.

32
00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:09.243
In the published simulations, NGF raised its receptor, TrkA, and lengthened each

33
00:02:09.243 --> 00:02:12.803
TrkA signal, and both were needed for the neurons to separate.

34
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:18.707
In the proposed model, winners then speed the death of losers, through a receptor

35
00:02:18.707 --> 00:02:20.027
called p75.

36
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.510
The rule has limits.

37
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.270
It does not say where a neuron's first small lead comes from.

38
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.080
Arriving early, branching more or sitting closer are all candidates.

39
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.540
The rule amplifies a head start.

40
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.660
Faster than in proportion is needed, not enough: steady basal synthesis pulls

41
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.800
neurons back together.

42
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.900
And the rule does not cover chick motor neurons.

43
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.720
They draw on several factors at once.

44
00:02:47.683 --> 00:02:52.023
Takeaway. A limited supply sets roughly how many survive.

45
00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:55.143
The neurons' own feedback decides which ones.
